Z||000000||speed racer||07-24-2001||02:05 AM||||My car is an 86 SE with a v-6 and also 30000 miles on it. Its running at about 220 or a little higher the fan will come on at about 2 marks above 220 and cool it back down to 220 again I have a 180 thermostat in it I just put it in I made sure that there was no air in the lines I was also going to change the radiator cap to see if its bad||||reg||4
Z||000001||Carrolles||07-24-2001||08:19 AM||||Make sure that you get a NONVENTED radiator cap not the one listed in AutpZone's book.  A VENTED cap will let coolant siphon into the overflow bottle when the car sits with the nose slightly down.  If the nose is down a lot you will lose most of your coolant.<P>I saw this phenomenon when I got my 85GT and jacked up the rar end to work on the engine.  Coolant steadily trickled through the overflow bottle onto the floor.<P>Do you know for sure that your water pump is pushing enough coolant?  They can wear out with time and the flow can be pretty low.<P>Do you run at 220 while driving at steady speed or does it only happen when you sit at idle?  Does the temp come back down when you turn the A/C on (this forces the radiator fan to come on)?<P>I have a stock 195 degree t-stat and consistently run around 190 or so.  When I sit at idle it will sometime rise.  Turning on the A/C always brings it back down.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||07-24-2001||10:11 AM||||Well, if you have the stock fan switch, it's gonna run warmer.  In traffic, et., the fan switch - not the thermo controls the engines temp.  Even without a thermo at all, in traffic the temp will continue to go up until the fan comes on - whatever temp that may be.  The only time it'll cool down lower is when you're moving.  To get the benefit of the 180 thermo, you need Rodney Dickman's low temp fan switch.  I put this and a 180 thermo in my Formula, and it stays between 180 and 200* no matter what.  It has never even gotten close to the 220* mark since I put it in!  I highly recommend it.||||reg||6
Z||000003||speed racer||07-24-2001||12:18 PM||||No I don’t know how good the water pump is.  That will be the last think I want to do though.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mach10||07-24-2001||01:58 PM||||Check fluid level. My car did EXACTLY the same thing. I had a bad pump that would plow water out the weep whenever it'd rev up. Never found ANYTHING on the driveways, but the cradle was always wet after a drive. I drove on a bad pump until I had a spare day. Just kept topping off the coolant.<BR>Pump was easy, but took a long time.||||reg||6
Z||000005||theogre||07-24-2001||08:02 PM||||There's likely nothing wrong with it. A cast iron motor running 210-220 is pretty normal.<P>The cycle you are seeing is the stock fan switch. It activates at 235F and shuts off at 225. Put in a low temperature switch if you want to pull it down closer to thermostat value.||||reg||1
